/-!
# The algebra of scope changes

A scope is read through existing carriers: a satisfaction relation between
programs and sentences, with a universe of admissible programs, a theory and
an observer (the theory–model framework), and the stages of the observer
presheaf.  This aggregate collects what a change of scope preserves.

* `Scope.Arrows`: four kinds of scope change (restrict, forget, identify,
  translate) as four shapes of one notion of translation, each with an exact
  preservation law and a control where the naive transfer fails.
* `Scope.UpdateSquares`: update squares; exact support and its algebra, the
  bridge to conservative extension of observer classes, nondeterministic
  updates (support is a kernel bisimulation), approximate squares with
  additive and amplified error, and three optimisation promises (exact,
  improvement, approximation).
* `Scope.ConsumerDescent`: which consumers descend along a forgetting; a
  choice-free descent criterion for dependent families along a quotient;
  histories with one answer and different cost or provenance; double counting
  of evidence; the thin view, where identity is proof-irrelevant.
* `Scope.PredicateDescent`: a predicate that does not descend has two liftings
  to views, its image and its universal image; they agree exactly on
  descending predicates.
* `Scope.Interaction`: forgetting commutes with translating exactly along
  bounded morphisms (Beck–Chevalley for the views), with restricting exactly
  on saturated fragments, and with identifying exactly for visible axioms.
* `Scope.Holonomy`: views glue on a connected index graph exactly when every
  cycle has trivial holonomy; sections and gluing are different obstructions.
* `Scope.HolonomyCover`: the disparity register (closed walks that move a
  value) is empty exactly when holonomy is trivial, for views with decidable
  equality; the holonomy cover, a
  covering of the index graph with unique walk lifting, glues the same views
  without identifying any value, while the orbit quotient is the finest
  quotient over the same index graph that glues and identifies two values at
  every disparity.
* `Scope.DisparitySearch`: on a connected index graph with a spanning tree,
  holonomy is trivial exactly when every fundamental cycle is, so disparities
  are generated by the fundamental cycles of the chords; spanning trees are
  built in rounds without choice; a decision procedure for finite systems
  checks fundamental cycles against values and returns either a gluing or a
  disparity as certificate; without decidable equality, a dovetailed,
  budgeted search is fair, reports only disparities it has confirmed, and
  eventually reports every disparity witnessed by apartness, and for a
  cotransitive apartness the fundamental cycles still suffice.
* `Scope.Authority`: selection by preference never grants admissibility,
  sealing composes, and programs that query only declared guarantees are
  invariant under guarantee-preserving changes.
-/
